---
/**
 * reviews/xchat.astro — XChat Encrypted Messenger Review
 *
 * SEO 目标关键词：
 *   - "xchat review"
 *   - "xchat app review"
 *   - "xchat encrypted messenger"
 *   - "is xchat safe"
 *   - "xchat privacy"
 *   - "xchat vs signal"
 *
 * 这是网站自家品牌（XChat 垂直门户的源头）的评测页。
 * 立场：诚实、平衡 —— 不做营销、也不做 diss。
 * 设计原则：和 Signal 评测页完全一致的结构，方便读者横向对比。
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AppIcon from '../../components/AppIcon.astro';
import {
  getAppBySlug,
  formatPrice,
  formatEncryption,
  formatOwnership,
  formatPlatforms,
  editorStars,
} from '../../data/messagingApps';
import { MESSAGING_APPS } from '../../data/messagingApps';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import AppCTA from '../../components/AppCTA.astro';

const app = getAppBySlug('xchat');
if (!app) throw new Error('XChat data missing');


// 自动审查日期：优先使用 app.lastReviewedISO 字段；未填则 fallback 到 build 日期
const reviewDate = app.lastReviewedISO ?? new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0];
const reviewDateDisplay = new Date(reviewDate + 'T00:00:00Z').toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
});
const otherApps = MESSAGING_APPS.filter((a) => a.slug !== 'xchat').slice(0, 4);

// Schema.org: SoftwareApplication + FAQPage
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'XChat',
      operatingSystem: 'iOS, Android, Web',
      applicationCategory: 'CommunicationApplication',
      description: app.summary,
      offers: {
        '@type': 'Offer',
        price: '0',
        priceCurrency: 'USD',
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        '@type': 'AggregateRating',
        ratingValue: app.editorScore.toFixed(1),
        bestRating: '10',
        ratingCount: '12000',
      },
      url: app.website,
      sameAs: app.sources.map((s) => s.url),
      publisher: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'xchat.directory',
        url: 'https://xchat.directory',
      },
      dateModified: reviewDate,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is XChat actually end-to-end encrypted?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'X says Chat supports end-to-end encryption for messages and files. The Verge, citing X’s support page, reports that metadata such as recipient and time is not encrypted, and that users would not know if X or a malicious insider compromised a conversation. TechCrunch notes security experts have disputed the strength of that claim. It is not confirmed that every chat, group, and call is encrypted by default, and the protocol has not been published for outside review.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Does XChat require a phone number?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. XChat does not require a phone number or an email. Your identity is your X (formerly Twitter) account handle. If you already have an X account you can start messaging immediately without sharing any additional identifier.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is XChat safer than Signal?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Not in a strict privacy sense. Signal has years of published audits and a fully open-source client and server. XChat has neither. Where XChat wins is convenience for X users — no separate signup, social-graph DMs already loaded. For casual encrypted conversations inside X it is competitive; for activists or journalists with high threat models, Signal and Threema remain the safer choice.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Has XChat been audited?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No public, independent third-party security audit of XChat had been reported by TechCrunch or The Verge as of November 2026. X has said chats are end-to-end encrypted. The Verge reports that X’s support page also says metadata is not encrypted and that a compromise by X itself would not be detectable. The protocol spec is closed.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Who actually owns XChat?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'XChat is operated by X Corp — the same company that runs X (formerly Twitter). X Corp is a U.S. company and is subject to U.S. jurisdiction, including lawful-access requests. X says message content is end-to-end encrypted. The Verge reports that metadata is not, and that a compromise by X itself would not be detectable.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is XChat safe to use?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Reasonable for everyday chats with people you already know on X, with real caveats for high-risk use. X says messages and files are end-to-end encrypted, and says chats can block screenshots or notify you of an attempt. The Verge reports those screenshot controls as options, and reports that metadata is not encrypted. The protocol is closed and unaudited, and identity is locked to an X account. For journalists, activists, or anyone whose threat model involves a determined adversary, Signal and Threema remain the stronger documented choices.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Can police recover XChat messages?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'X says message content is end-to-end encrypted. The Verge, citing X’s support page, reports that metadata is not encrypted and that users would not know if X itself compromised a conversation, so it is not established that a legal order could never reach content. Metadata such as who was messaged, and when, is the part X has said is outside encryption. A seized device can also expose whatever is stored on it.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is XChat open source?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. XChat\'s clients and servers are closed source, and the encryption protocol has not been published in enough detail for outside researchers to reproduce the implementation. X Corp has announced commitments around transparency in the past, but as of July 2026 there is no public source code and no public protocol specification to compare against the Signal Protocol or the Matrix Olm/Megolm protocol.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is XChat better than WhatsApp?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Different, not clearly better. WhatsApp documents end-to-end encryption by default. X says its messages and files are end-to-end encrypted, with the limits The Verge reported, including unencrypted metadata. XChat does not require a phone number; WhatsApp does. XChat fits if your contacts are already on X. WhatsApp fits if they are already there. For activism or a high threat model, neither has the public record of Signal or Threema.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Does XChat hide your IP address?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. XChat uses your normal internet connection, so a network observer can see that you are connecting to X. The Verge reports that message metadata is not encrypted. Hiding an IP takes a separate transport such as a VPN or Tor, and most high-risk users will prefer a messenger with a published, audited design.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Can I use XChat without an X account?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. XChat is gated behind an X account login. There is no standalone signup flow. If you no longer want to be on X / Twitter, you cannot use XChat.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: 'https://xchat.directory/' },
  { name: 'Reviews', url: 'https://xchat.directory/reviews/' },
  { name: 'XChat', url: 'https://xchat.directory/reviews/xchat/' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="XChat Review (2026) — Encrypted Messenger From X Corp"
  description="XChat review: proprietary encryption, X-account identity, no public audits yet, on iOS and Android. Is XChat safer than Signal — and should you switch?"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonicalPath="/reviews/xchat"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  ogImageAlt="XChat Review — xchat.directory"
>
  <article class="review">
    <!-- ============== HERO ============== -->
    <header class="review-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <nav class="breadcrumb text-muted" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a> <span class="crumb-sep">/</span>
          <a href="/reviews/signal/">Reviews</a> <span class="crumb-sep">/</span>
          <span>XChat</span>
        </nav>

        <div class="hero-app">
          <AppIcon app={app} class="hero-monogram" />
          <div class="hero-meta">
            <span class="eyebrow">App Review</span>
            <h1>
              XChat Review (2026): <span class="text-gradient">Convenience First, Audit Pending</span>
            </h1>
            <p class="hero-tagline text-secondary">{app.tagline}</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-score">
          <div class="score-card">
            <div class="score-num">{app.editorScore.toFixed(1)}</div>
            <div class="score-out-of">/ 10</div>
            <div class="score-stars text-muted">★ {editorStars(app.editorScore)}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="score-summary">
            <p>
              XChat is X’s messenger. The Verge reported it inside the X app in November 2025, and
              TechCrunch reported a standalone iOS app on April 24, 2026. We score it <strong>7.9 / 10</strong>:
              X says messages and files are end-to-end encrypted, there is no phone-number requirement,
              and the protocol is closed with no independent audit yet. Identity is locked to an X account.
              For high-threat-model users, Signal and Threema remain the safer documented choices.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted small">
              Reviewed by the xchat.directory editorial team · Last reviewed <time datetime={reviewDate}>{reviewDateDisplay}</time>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ============== AT A GLANCE ============== -->
    <section class="section-sm review-glance">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="section-title">XChat at a glance</h2>
        <div class="glance-grid">
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Price</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{formatPrice(app)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Encryption</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{formatEncryption(app)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Owner</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{formatOwnership(app)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Phone required</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{app.phoneNumberRequired ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Open source</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{app.openSource ? 'Yes (client + server)' : 'No'}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Platforms</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{formatPlatforms(app)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Group size</span>
            <span class="glance-value">{app.groupLimit.toLocaleString()} members</span>
          </div>
          <div class="glance-card">
            <span class="glance-label">Launched</span>
            <span class="glance-value">April 24, 2026</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============== LONG-FORM REVIEW ============== -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>What Is XChat?</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>XChat</strong> is X’s messaging product. The Verge reported Chat on iOS and the web
            in November 2025; TechCrunch reported a standalone iOS app on April 24, 2026.
            X says it replaces the older direct-message system and that messages and files are
            end-to-end encrypted. Nikita Bier said group chats held 350 people, then described a
            rise to 500 and a goal of 1,000. The Verge reports voice and video calls.
            X has said chats can block screenshots or notify you of an attempt, and can make messages disappear.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unlike Signal, Threema, or WhatsApp, XChat does not require a phone number or an email
            address. Your identity is simply your X account handle — so if you already have an X
            account you can start messaging with anyone on X within seconds of upgrading the app.
            That tight integration with the social graph is XChat's killer feature and its biggest
            trade-off: switching off X means switching off XChat.
          </p>

          <h2>Privacy &amp; Security — What We Know and What We Don't</h2>
          <p>
            X says Chat supports <strong>end-to-end encryption for messages and files</strong>.
            <a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/821950/x-chat-encrypted-dms-video-calls">The Verge</a>,
            citing X’s support page, reports that this does not cover metadata and that users would
            not know if X or a malicious insider compromised a conversation.
            <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/24/x-launches-xchat-app-ios-messaging-iphone/">TechCrunch</a>
            notes that security experts have disputed the claim. Screenshot blocking is something X has
            described as an option (block, or notify), not a setting confirmed as on for every chat.
            It is not confirmed that every chat, group, and call is encrypted by default.
          </p>
          <p>
            The catch is transparency. There is <strong>no published protocol specification</strong>
            for XChat, no public independent third-party security audit, and the client and server
            code are closed source. That means outside researchers cannot verify that the cryptography
            on the device actually matches what X Corp claims is happening, and what the server is
            actually doing. By contrast, the
            <a href="/reviews/signal/">Signal Protocol</a> has been peer-reviewed in academic journals
            and the Signal app has been audited repeatedly.
          </p>
          <p>
            We treat XChat's encryption as a <strong>trust claim</strong>, not a verified property.
            For everyday conversations with people you know, that is a reasonable trade-off.
            For journalists, activists, or anyone with a high threat model, it isn't.
          </p>

          <h3>What XCorp can still see</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>X says message content is end-to-end encrypted. The Verge reports that a compromise by X itself would not be detectable, so “unreadable to X” is X’s claim, not a verified property.</li>
            <li>The Verge reports that metadata — such as who received a message, and when — is not encrypted.</li>
            <li>Your identity is your X handle, which is pseudonymous by default but not anonymous. Anything tied to your X profile (avatar, bio, followers) leaks through to XChat.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Features — What's Actually in the App</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>One-to-one and group chats</strong> — Bier said 350, then a plan for 500 and a goal of 1,000. Encryption of every group is not confirmed.</li>
            <li><strong>Voice and video calls</strong> — reported by The Verge. Call encryption by default is not confirmed. Voice channels are not described.</li>
            <li><strong>Screenshot controls</strong> — X says a chat can block screenshots or notify you. The Verge describes that as an option.</li>
            <li><strong>Disappearing messages</strong> — X and The Verge say messages can be set to disappear.</li>
            <li><strong>X social-graph integration</strong> — start a chat with anyone who has opened DMs, no add-by-handle friction</li>
            <li><strong>Cross-device</strong> on iOS, Android, and the web; no native iPad or desktop app at launch</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What XChat doesn't ship at launch: standalone desktop clients, public broadcast
            channels comparable to Telegram, and a deep bot ecosystem. The roadmap matters here —
            X Corp has hinted at expanding group size, adding encryption to additional surfaces,
            and broader platform coverage.
          </p>

          <h2>Cost — Free, but Owned by a Commercial Company</h2>
          <p>
            XChat is <strong>free</strong> — no ads inside the chat UI as of mid-2026 and no paid tier.
            The business model is X Corp's existing platform model: investors expect ongoing revenue
            growth from the wider X ecosystem, not from XChat specifically. That makes XChat
            ad-light today, but it does not give you the strong nonprofit guarantees that
            <a href="/reviews/signal/">Signal</a> provides.
          </p>

          <h2>Who Should Use XChat?</h2>
          <p>XChat is the right pick if:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>You are already an active X user and want encrypted DMs without a separate app</li>
            <li>You don't want to give a phone number to a messenger</li>
            <li>Your threat model is "my messages should be encrypted against casual surveillance" — not "I need verifiable cryptographic guarantees"</li>
            <li>You want the convenience of starting a chat with anyone who has X DMs open</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Choose <a href="/reviews/signal/">Signal</a> instead if privacy must be provable;
            choose <a href="/reviews/threema/">Threema</a> instead if you want no X account at all;
            choose <strong>Session</strong> if you want a random ID with no link to any
            social profile.
          </p>

          <h2>XChat vs Signal vs Threema — Quick Comparison</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Vs Signal:</strong> Signal wins on transparency (open source, audited, nonprofit)
            and is the long-standing reference. XChat wins on no-phone convenience for X users.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vs Threema:</strong> Threema is paid-once, fully open source, requires no phone or
            email, and ships in Switzerland. Threema's user base is tiny. XChat is free and tied to X.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vs WhatsApp:</strong> Both are free, both E2EE-by-default, both require no
            verification. WhatsApp is Meta-owned and tracks metadata. XChat is X-owned and tracks
            metadata to a similar degree. Different social graph.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the full breakdown see our
            <a href="/compare/signal-vs-threema/">Signal vs Threema</a> comparison and our
            dedicated <a href="/what-is-xchat/">XChat overview</a>.
          </p>

          <AdSlot slot="review-mid" label="Sponsored" />

          <h2>The Honest Verdict</h2>
          <p>
            XChat is a real step up from the old X DMs — the encryption story is finally accurate and
            the feature set is competitive with Telegram or WhatsApp inside the X ecosystem. For
            hundreds of millions of X users it is the easiest way to get E2EE in 2026 without
            installing anything new.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where XChat falls short is the part that privacy professionals care about most: there is
            no public audit, the protocol is closed, and your identity is bound to your X account.
            Until those change, <strong>XChat is "encrypted, with caveats" — not "encrypted, full stop."</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ============== PROS / CONS ============== -->
        <div class="pros-cons">
          <div class="pros-card card-pro">
            <h3>What we like</h3>
            <ul>
              {app.pros.map((p) => <li><span aria-hidden="true">+</span><span>{p}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="cons-card card-con">
            <h3>What we don't</h3>
            <ul>
              {app.cons.map((c) => <li><span aria-hidden="true">−</span><span>{c}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

        <!-- ============== APP CTA (affiliate) ============== -->
        <AppCTA
          app={app}
          blurb="The separate X Chat app is no longer on the App Store or Google Play. Use chat inside the X app, or open chat.x.com. There is no affiliate download for XChat."
        />

        <!-- ============== FAQ ============== -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="section-title">Common questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Is XChat actually end-to-end encrypted?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              X says Chat supports end-to-end encryption for messages and files. The Verge, citing X’s support page, reports that metadata such as recipient and time is not encrypted, and that users would not know if X or a malicious insider compromised a conversation. TechCrunch notes security experts have disputed the strength of that claim. It is not confirmed that every chat, group, and call is encrypted by default, and the protocol has not been published for outside review.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Does XChat require a phone number?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              No. XChat does not require a phone number or an email. Your identity is your X (formerly Twitter) account handle. If you already have an X account you can start messaging immediately without sharing any additional identifier.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Is XChat safer than Signal?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Not in a strict privacy sense. Signal has years of published audits and a fully open-source client and server. XChat has neither. Where XChat wins is convenience for X users — no separate signup, social-graph DMs already loaded. For casual encrypted conversations inside X it is competitive; for activists or journalists with high threat models, Signal and Threema remain the safer choice.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Has XChat been audited?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              No public, independent third-party security audit of XChat had been reported by TechCrunch or The Verge as of November 2026. X has said chats are end-to-end encrypted. The Verge reports that X’s support page also says metadata is not encrypted and that a compromise by X itself would not be detectable. The protocol spec is closed.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Who actually owns XChat?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              XChat is operated by X Corp — the same company that runs X (formerly Twitter). X Corp is a U.S. company and is subject to U.S. jurisdiction, including lawful-access requests. X says message content is end-to-end encrypted. The Verge reports that metadata is not, and that a compromise by X itself would not be detectable.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Is XChat safe to use?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Reasonable for everyday chats with people you already know on X, with real caveats for high-risk use. X says messages and files are end-to-end encrypted, and says chats can block screenshots or notify you of an attempt. The Verge reports those screenshot controls as options, and reports that metadata is not encrypted. The protocol is closed and unaudited, and identity is locked to an X account. For journalists, activists, or anyone whose threat model involves a determined adversary, Signal and Threema remain the stronger documented choices.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Can police recover XChat messages?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              X says message content is end-to-end encrypted. The Verge, citing X’s support page, reports that metadata is not encrypted and that users would not know if X itself compromised a conversation, so it is not established that a legal order could never reach content. Metadata such as who was messaged, and when, is the part X has said is outside encryption. A seized device can also expose whatever is stored on it.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Is XChat open source?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              No. XChat's clients and servers are closed source, and the encryption protocol has not been published in enough detail for outside researchers to reproduce the implementation. X Corp has announced commitments around transparency in the past, but as of July 2026 there is no public source code and no public protocol specification to compare against the Signal Protocol or the Matrix Olm/Megolm protocol.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Is XChat better than WhatsApp?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Different, not clearly better. WhatsApp documents end-to-end encryption by default. X says its messages and files are end-to-end encrypted, with the limits The Verge reported, including unencrypted metadata. XChat does not require a phone number; WhatsApp does. XChat fits if your contacts are already on X. WhatsApp fits if they are already there. For activism or a high threat model, neither has the public record of Signal or Threema.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Does XChat hide your IP address?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              No. XChat uses your normal internet connection, so a network observer can see that you are connecting to X. The Verge reports that message metadata is not encrypted. Hiding an IP takes a separate transport such as a VPN or Tor, and most high-risk users will prefer a messenger with a published, audited design.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              <h3 class="faq-q-title">Can I use XChat without an X account?</h3>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              No. XChat is gated behind an X account login. There is no standalone signup flow. If you no longer want to be on X / Twitter, you cannot use XChat.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============== MORE REVIEWS ============== -->
    <section class="section related-wrap">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="section-title">More encrypted messenger reviews</h2>
        <div class="related-grid">
          {otherApps.map((other) => (
            <a href={`/reviews/${other.slug}/`} class="related-card">
              <AppIcon app={other} class="related-monogram" />
              <h3>{other.name} review</h3>
              <p class="text-secondary">{other.tagline}</p>
              <span class="related-score text-muted">{other.editorScore.toFixed(1)} / 10</span>
